1. Get explicit written authorization and define scope (hosts, apps, endpoints, time window), plus an emergency stop procedure and contact list.  
2. Inventory your current edge stack (CDN/reverse proxy, rule engine, managed rulesets, custom rules) and export the full policy/config so you can diff changes and roll back quickly.  
3. Turn on full security telemetry: edge access logs, rule/audit logs, upstream app logs, and a single correlation ID propagated from edge → app (e.g., `X-Request-ID`).  
4. Establish a safe test harness that sends **non-destructive** requests: use a staging environment or production read-only endpoints, rate-limited from a dedicated source IP range.  
5. Build an automated regression suite from well-known defensive test cases (e.g., OWASP CRS regression tests) and your own historical incident indicators; run it on every rule/config change in CI.  
6. Add canonicalization/normalization on the server side before validation (decode once, normalize unicode, collapse path segments) and validate against strict schemas so obfuscation does not change meaning.  
7. Enforce strict input validation per endpoint (types, min/max lengths, allowed characters, allowed enums) and reject anything outside the contract with a uniform error response. Example (Node/Express + schema validation):  
   ```js
   import express from "express";
   import rateLimit from "express-rate-limit";
   import helmet from "helmet";
   import { z } from "zod";

   const app = express();
   app.use(helmet());
   app.use(express.json({ limit: "64kb" }));

   app.use(rateLimit({
     windowMs: 60_000,
     max: 120,
     standardHeaders: true,
     legacyHeaders: false
   }));

   const createUserSchema = z.object({
     email: z.string().email().max(254),
     name: z.string().min(1).max(80).regex(/^[a-zA-Z .'-]+$/),
     age: z.number().int().min(13).max(130)
   });

   app.post("/api/users", (req, res) => {
     const parsed = createUserSchema.safeParse(req.body);
     if (!parsed.success) return res.status(400).json({ error: "invalid_request" });
     // proceed with parsed.data only
     res.status(201).json({ ok: true });
   });
   ```
8. Eliminate injection primitives in the app: use parameterized queries/ORM, forbid dynamic SQL/NoSQL string concatenation, and use safe command execution APIs with fixed argument lists.  
9. Add response-side protections: consistent error messages, no stack traces, security headers (CSP, HSTS, X-Content-Type-Options), and robust output encoding in templates to prevent script injection.  
10. Tune edge rules using anomaly scoring rather than single-string signatures; maintain allowlists per endpoint/parameter and blocklists for clearly malicious patterns only when confirmed by logs.  
11. Introduce rate controls and abuse mitigations beyond simple per-IP limits: token bucket per account/session, bot signals, device fingerprinting (where appropriate), and challenge/step-up for suspicious behavior.  
12. Create a change-management loop: (a) propose rule change, (b) run regression suite, (c) deploy in detection-only mode, (d) review false positives/negatives, (e) enforce, (f) monitor KPIs (block rate, FP rate, latency).  
13. Add continuous monitoring and alerting: thresholds on blocked requests, spikes by endpoint, top rule triggers, and “near-miss” events; route alerts to on-call with runbooks for triage and rollback.  
14. Document and operationalize: maintain an endpoint-by-endpoint contract, validation schemas, and an incident playbook; schedule quarterly reviews to retire unused rules and update the ruleset baseline.
